/* Wealth Lab · Module 7 Economics and Financial Mathematics · Academic addon 2026-11-02 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM7Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m7-academic-v1","version":"academic-addon-3.0.0","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","lessonIds":["L7.S1","L7.S2","L7.S3","L7.S4"],"lessons":[{"id":"L7.S1","title":"Inflation and Real Wealth","subtitle":"Price indexes, purchasing power, real returns, wages, housing and inflation-sensitive assets","prerequisites":["L1.S1 net worth and liquidity","L1.S3 compound growth","L4.S2 risk and portfolio horizons"],"objectives":["Distinguish price level, inflation, disinflation and deflation","Construct and interpret a CPI index and household-specific basket","Compute exact real return and purchasing-power transformations","Evaluate real wages, cash drag and home-purchase affordability","Assess inflation expectations, TIPS mechanics and basis risk","Build uncertainty and inflation stress cases for a family savings plan"],"sections":[{"title":"Inflation measures a changing price level, not the price of every item","body":["An index tracks the weighted prices of a defined set of goods and services relative to an index base. The US Bureau of Labor Statistics publishes the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (CPI-U) and other indexes, each with its own coverage, weights, methodology and publication schedule. One monthly price increase is not an annual inflation rate. A rising index means the average prices in the basket have risen under the method; a family's actual expenses can diverge because its purchases and geography differ.","Inflation is a positive rate of change in a price index. Disinflation means the positive rate slows—for example, prices grow 5% one year and 3% the next, so the price level keeps rising. Deflation means the broad price index declines. Economists must also distinguish a one-time relative-price change from ongoing general inflation. Rent, groceries and insurance may behave very differently across households; an index is an economic measurement, not an individual budget statement.","Compute an exact one-period inflation rate as CPI_end/CPI_start−1. If a hypothetical index moves from 300 to 312, the change is 312/300−1=4%. If it rises another 3% in the following year, the index becomes 321.36, and two-year cumulative inflation is 321.36/300−1=7.12%, not 7% exactly. Frequency and compounding matter in both inflation and investment returns.","The CPI is estimated using sampled prices and statistical methods; its basket and quality adjustments can affect long-run comparison. Critiques of an index should ask what population, consumption weights, substitution assumptions and inflation concept it measures, not simply label every difference a falsification. For a household saving for a Connecticut home, a separate construction-cost, mortgage-rate and local property-tax model may be more useful than predicting house prices using the national CPI."],"worked":["Hypothetical CPI 300.00 to 312.00 ⇒ inflation (312/300−1)×100 =4%.","Second year grows by 3% ⇒ CPI 312×1.03=321.36.","Two-year cumulative inflation 321.36/300−1=7.12%.","Disinflation from 4% to 3% still increases prices; deflation would require a negative index change.","Local rent, house prices and insurance may move differently from CPI."],"practice":[["If inflation declines from 6% to 2%, what has happened to the overall price level?","It generally continues rising, just more slowly. This is disinflation, not necessarily deflation or restoration of the original purchasing power."],["Why is a national CPI index not an exact forecast for an individual Connecticut household's expenses?","Consumption weights, location, product selection and exposure to housing, energy, medical and insurance costs differ from the index's representative population."]]},{"title":"Personal inflation and the construction of a weighted cost index","body":["A household can estimate the inflation it experienced by comparing comparable purchases over time and weighting the categories by their consumption amounts. A simple current-basket estimate uses price changes multiplied by starting expenditure weights. However, if the household switches brands, moves, changes consumption amounts or faces rent increases unrelated to CPI, the estimate reflects both inflation and choices. This is why researchers distinguish fixed-weight Laspeyres-type measures, substitution and chained measures.","Suppose annual spending in a fictional household begins with 45% housing, 20% food, 15% transportation, 10% utilities and 10% other. Assume category prices move 6%, 3%, −2%, 8% and 4%, respectively. A simple fixed-weight rate is .45×6%+.20×3%+.15×(−2%)+.10×8%+.10×4%=4.2%. The household's expense basket grew faster than a hypothetical 3% national CPI, even though gasoline-related transport prices fell.","Changing weights matters. Paying off a car loan, adding a dependent or buying a home changes exposure to prices; a fixed basket becomes less representative with time. An owner who takes a fixed-rate mortgage freezes the contractual principal-and-interest payment but not property taxes, maintenance or insurance. A renter may face direct rent resets. Comparing nominal balances without tracking the family's costs can create a false sense of rising wealth.","For budgeting, write a category-level inflation stress with best-case, base-case and adverse-case assumptions. Prioritize unavoidable items and keep projected wages or investment returns independent of spending-growth assumptions. The correct decision might be to increase emergency reserves or delay a purchase rather than chase a speculative investment expected to beat the average inflation statistic."],"worked":["Starting category weights: housing 45%, food 20%, transport 15%, utilities 10%, other 10%.","Category price changes 6%, 3%, −2%, 8%, 4%.","Weighted inflation .45×.06+.20×.03+.15×(−.02)+.10×.08+.10×.04=.042=4.2%.","If starting total monthly expenses $3,000 and quantities unchanged, comparable basket cost rises to $3,126.","This is an illustrative fixed-basket index, not a prediction of actual spending."],"practice":[["Why can changing groceries from a premium store to discount brands obscure a naive measured personal inflation rate?","Observed spending changes reflect substitution, quantities and quality as well as like-for-like price changes; an accurate personal inflation estimate separates those effects."],["How can a fixed-rate mortgage still expose a homeowner to inflation?","Taxes, insurance, utilities, repairs and maintenance are separate from contractually fixed principal and interest; their costs can increase."]]},{"title":"Exact nominal-versus-real return and the Fisher transformation","body":["Nominal investment return is the percentage change in stated dollars, before purchasing-power adjustment. Real return is the return measured in units of the price-index basket. The exact relation is 1+r_real=(1+r_nominal)/(1+π), where π is the index inflation rate over the same period. The common approximation r_nominal−π neglects a cross term; it is useful for small rates but incorrect in high-inflation or long-horizon calculations.","Suppose an account earns 6% in nominal dollars during a year when inflation is 3%. Exact real return is 1.06/1.03−1≈2.9126%, rather than exactly 3%. If a savings account earns 2% while inflation is 5%, real return is 1.02/1.05−1≈−2.8571%. A balance can grow in dollars while losing spending power. This is not evidence that the bank stole cash; it identifies the opportunity and purchasing-power cost of holding a particular asset.","Model gross and net results separately. Fees reduce nominal growth, and taxes on interest may reduce spendable return. If gross nominal return is 6%, a hypothetical 1% fee drag and effective current tax cost are not necessarily precisely additive; determine actual net factor before converting to real wealth. Investment taxes depend on account type, income character and realized events. A risky stock does not guarantee a positive real return just because its historical nominal average exceeded inflation.","Money needed at a specific date has two constraints: amount and liquidity. A three-month closing reserve should not be invested in equities merely because long-run expected real returns are higher. Wealth Lab should let a learner stress a household's purchasing power without converting an expected return into a guaranteed finance source."],"worked":["Nominal 6% and inflation 3% ⇒ exact real 1.06/1.03−1=2.9126%.","Nominal 2% and inflation 5% ⇒ exact real 1.02/1.05−1=−2.8571%.","On $10,000, nominal 2% end amount $10,200; in initial purchasing-power units $10,200/1.05≈$9,714.29.","Real wealth declines about $285.71 even while the dollar balance grows by $200.","The simple nominal-minus-inflation approximation should be labeled approximate."],"practice":[["Why does a 6% investment gain during 3% inflation produce a real return slightly below 3%?","The exact conversion divides growth factors, 1.06/1.03−1≈2.9126%, rather than subtracting percentage rates."],["What additional variables do you need before treating an investment's gross nominal result as spendable real wealth?","Fees, applicable taxes and timing of cash flows, the correct inflation index and liquidity constraints can materially change the spendable result."]]},{"title":"Real wages, brackets and the gap between salary raises and living costs","body":["Household income is adequate only relative to taxes, debts and expenses. A nominal raise can coincide with declining real pay. If a worker receives a 3% wage increase but their household consumption basket costs 5% more, the exact change in real wages is 1.03/1.05−1≈−1.9048%. The new paycheck is larger, but it buys less of the prior basket. Wage changes also affect benefit eligibility, tax withholding and retirement contribution capacity, which should be assessed separately.","Inflation interacts with taxation. Certain US federal thresholds are indexed for inflation, but not all limits and rules adjust identically, and states use separate laws. Nominal capital gains may contain substantial inflation components even if the investor's real appreciation is small. A household should therefore distinguish nominal pretax wealth, after-tax wealth and purchasing power of after-tax assets. A $250 monthly savings goal may require periodic increases in deposits to maintain the same real contribution over 20 years.","Model a simple pay increase: initial annual income $50,000, nominal raise 3%, actual CPI inflation 5%. New salary is $51,500, and its price-level-adjusted purchasing power is $51,500/1.05≈$49,047.62 in last year's dollars. The real equivalent is about $952.38 below the prior $50,000. It is incorrect to infer that the person is financially better off simply from the nominal $1,500 raise.","Real-income analysis should include any shift in hours, employment stability, insurance premiums, rent and commuting cost. The average CPI and wage growth are aggregate indicators, while a household's real financial condition is an individual cash-flow calculation. A durable investing plan establishes an affordable base contribution rather than requiring wage growth to automatically exceed future inflation."],"worked":["Initial annual gross wage $50,000; 3% nominal raise ⇒ new wage $51,500.","Inflation 5% ⇒ real current salary in prior-year units $51,500/1.05≈$49,047.62.","Real wage change 1.03/1.05−1≈−1.9048%.","Nominal wage increased $1,500 but purchasing-power equivalent declined about $952.38.","Taxes and spending composition require separate analyses."],"practice":[["Why can a pay rise appear positive on a W-2 while the worker's real living standard falls?","Prices for the worker's purchase basket can increase more quickly than nominal pay, and after-tax/expense outcomes may differ from gross salary."],["Should an investor automatically increase risk in order to keep up with inflation?","No. Risk capacity, time horizon, debt and essential cash needs govern feasible investments; higher expected growth never guarantees inflation protection."]]},{"title":"Inflation-sensitive bonds, TIPS, market risk and break-even inflation","body":["Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) differ from nominal Treasuries because principal is adjusted under specified CPI-based rules, with coupons calculated from adjusted principal. TreasuryDirect explains that TIPS mature in five, ten or thirty years under currently offered term categories and pay at least original par principal at maturity, subject to US Treasury terms. Their secondary-market prices can fall before maturity, and purchasing before a market yield increase can generate an interim loss even though the principal adjustment mechanism operates.","A common nominal-versus-real yield comparison is the approximate break-even inflation rate: nominal Treasury yield minus comparable-maturity real TIPS yield. For a simplified illustrative 10-year nominal yield of 4.5% and real yield of 2%, approximate break-even inflation is 2.5%. A more precise compounded factor comparison is 1.045/1.02−1≈2.451%. In real markets, liquidity differences, risk premiums, indexation lag and market structure mean this spread is not a clean, guaranteed forecast of average future CPI inflation.","Interest-rate risk still matters. A real-yield increase depresses the present value of fixed real future cash flows, so a TIPS fund may lose market value when real yields rise. A single TIPS held through maturity and a TIPS fund that continually buys securities provide different cash-flow guarantees. Tax treatment of inflation adjustments can also matter in taxable accounts even when the adjustment is not distributed immediately in cash.","For a home down payment due soon, matching cash maturity and account access normally matters more than buying long-duration inflation exposure. The lesson should compare ordinary Treasury bills, bank deposits and eligible insured accounts, individual TIPS and TIPS mutual funds by maturity, liquidity, after-tax outcomes and interim price risks. It should never state 'TIPS cannot lose money' without specifying holding and maturity conditions."],"worked":["Illustrative 10-year nominal yield 4.5%, TIPS real yield 2.0%.","Approximate break-even inflation 4.5%−2.0%=2.5 percentage points.","Exact factor conversion 1.045/1.02−1≈2.451%.","If an inflation-indexed principal is $1,000 and its index ratio rises to 1.08, adjusted principal becomes $1,080 in this hypothetical.","A TIPS fund may still lose market price when real yields rise."],"practice":[["Does the principal floor at TIPS maturity mean an investor who sells a TIPS fund early cannot lose money?","No. Secondary-market prices and fund NAV can fluctuate with real interest rates, liquidity and fees; the maturity floor applies to the individual security under its terms."],["Why isn't nominal-minus-TIPS yield spread a perfect inflation forecast?","Term, liquidity and inflation risk premiums plus CPI index conventions and market frictions affect yields; break-even is market-implied pricing, not a guaranteed future CPI result."]]},{"title":"Inflation, real housing wealth and mortgage leverage","body":["A home is a real asset but it is not a guaranteed inflation hedge. Sale price depends on local supply, demand, credit rates, condition, taxes and transaction costs. A fixed nominal mortgage may become easier to service if income increases while its payment stays fixed, but wage growth is not automatic and real mortgage affordability also depends on insurance, property taxes, repair costs and refinancing opportunities. Inflation can simultaneously increase home-replacement expenses and interest rates on new borrowing.","Consider a $250,000 house financed with a $237,500 mortgage and $12,500 initial owner equity, ignoring closing costs. If the home's nominal price rises 3% to $257,500 in one year and loan principal is unchanged for an isolated illustration, nominal home equity becomes $20,000. If inflation over the year was 5%, the home's real price in prior-year dollars is $257,500/1.05≈$245,238, a real decline in the asset's value despite nominal appreciation. Mortgage repayment and property expenses must be modeled separately.","Leverage increases sensitivity of equity to house value. A 10% price decline on a $250,000 home produces a $25,000 decline in property value; with unchanged $237,500 mortgage, equity falls from $12,500 to negative $12,500 before costs. This 200% loss relative to starting equity reflects financial leverage, not evidence that real estate always behaves like an investment fund. A purchase with $12,500 down may require more liquid cash for closing, repair reserves and potential sale.","When evaluating inheritance, children may receive a property subject to mortgage and taxes, not necessarily $250,000 of unrestricted cash. The economic plan should compare estimated selling price, mortgage payoff, sale expenses, relevant tax basis and the family's ability to carry monthly expenses. Inflation, loan terms and estate structure each have their own ledger."],"worked":["Hypothetical $250,000 home, outstanding $237,500 mortgage ⇒ $12,500 equity initially.","Nominal home growth 3% ⇒ $257,500 asset and $20,000 equity if mortgage unchanged.","5% inflation ⇒ real home asset value $257,500/1.05≈$245,238.","A 10% home price fall to $225,000 ⇒ negative $12,500 equity before closing costs.","Transaction expenses and debt paydowns must be added to real-world projections."],"practice":[["Why is real estate not automatically a safe inflation hedge for a highly leveraged household?","Local prices may decline while the nominal mortgage and property bills remain; leverage magnifies equity losses and selling costs can further reduce proceeds."],["Why should an heir not describe a $250,000 mortgaged house as a $250,000 cash inheritance?","Debt, sale costs, carrying expenses and legal rights may substantially reduce recoverable proceeds, and home value is uncertain until a transaction occurs."]]},{"title":"Long-run goals: nominal targets versus purchasing-power targets","body":["Saving for a distant objective requires separating the future nominal bill from its present purchasing-power equivalent. If an education expense costs $30,000 today and rises 3% annually, its modeled nominal expense in ten years is $30,000×1.03^10≈$40,317.48. If financial investments grow 6% nominally each year, their future dollars must be compared with the correctly inflated expense, not the original $30,000 benchmark. A forecast must also include fees, tax treatment and uncertain return sequences.","Constant-contribution annuity models can mislead when wage and expense inflation differ. Investing $250 at each month-end for twenty years at a smooth hypothetical 6% nominal annual return compounded monthly gives about $115,510.22, from $60,000 contributions; the remaining balance is modeled growth before taxes, fees and inflation. With 3% inflation, its present purchasing-power equivalent is only about $63,896, and actual stock and bond performance will not follow a smooth monthly path.","An inflation-indexed contribution plan raises deposits as the target's expected nominal cost changes. Yet cash-flow affordability is a constraint: a person cannot save larger amounts if monthly bills use all available income. Robust planning uses a low-return, middle-return and loss scenario, then asks what decisions still work when the adverse scenario occurs. The highest net-worth forecast is not necessarily the safest funding strategy for a dated expense.","The concluding policy is an inflation-adjusted household scorecard: nominal assets; liabilities and loan structure; annual projected expenses by category; real value of liquid reserves; goals by due date; after-tax and after-fee investments; and a review rule when household inflation or salary growth deviates materially from expectations. This provides a bridge to monetary policy, compounding, bond duration and economic indicator lessons."],"worked":["Today's $30,000 future expense at 3% for ten years ⇒ $40,317.49.","Twenty years of $250 month-end savings at 6% nominal / 12 ⇒ approximately $115,510.22.","Total contributions $250×240=$60,000; hypothetical growth $55,510.22.","At 3% annual inflation, terminal purchasing-power equivalent ≈$115,510.22/1.03^20.","This calculation assumes a smooth deterministic return that is not a market prediction."],"practice":[["Why must the nominal savings forecast for a ten-year education goal be compared with the projected nominal education cost?","Comparing different date and price-level units understates the needed target; bring assets and expenses to the same future date or to common present-value units."],["What is a prudent response if future household expenses rise faster than the assumed general CPI?","Revise the household-specific budget and funding projections, consider increasing savings only when affordable, and protect dated obligations instead of forcing higher investment risk."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["BLS Consumer Price Index","https://www.bls.gov/cpi/"],["BLS CPI Frequently Asked Questions","https://www.bls.gov/cpi/questions-and-answers.htm"],["TreasuryDirect TIPS and inflation principal adjustments","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/tips/"],["TreasuryDirect Treasury security price/yield","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/understanding-pricing/"],["Federal Reserve Economic Data / series documentation","https://fred.stlouisfed.org/"],["Investor.gov inflation and risk basics","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing"]],"assessment":{"title":"Inflation-indexed home-and-investment policy","prompt":"Construct a household CPI basket and compare it with published CPI, compute exact real wages and investment returns, evaluate individual TIPS versus funds, and stress a leveraged $250,000 home with a $237,500 loan under 3% nominal growth, 5% price inflation and 10% market decline. Fund a $30,000 goal due in ten years. Document tax, liquidity, income and price-index assumptions.","rubric":["Price-index construction and inflation interpretation (20%)","Exact real/nominal computations and cash-flow timing (25%)","Inflation instrument and yield-spread analysis (20%)","Housing leverage and goal stress tests (20%)","Primary sources, caveats and evidence-based decision (15%)"]}},{"id":"L7.S2","title":"Rates, Central Banks & Bonds","subtitle":"Monetary policy transmission, yield curves, Treasury pricing, duration and mortgage-rate risk","prerequisites":["L7.S1 inflation and real returns","L4.S1 debt versus ownership claims","L4.S2 interest-rate risk"],"objectives":["Identify FOMC objectives and actual operational instruments","Explain overnight policy transmission without confusing mortgage pricing","Decompose nominal yields into term, inflation and credit components","Price discounted fixed and zero-coupon cash flows","Calculate duration sensitivity and convexity caveats","Compare mortgage rates, APR, points and refinance breakeven","Distinguish fixed, floating, indexed and default-sensitive securities"],"sections":[{"title":"Federal Reserve mandate, the FOMC and the money-market implementation layer","body":["The Federal Reserve's monetary policy mandate emphasizes maximum employment and price stability. The Federal Open Market Committee sets a target range for the federal funds rate, an overnight interbank funding rate, rather than setting every consumer credit contract. The Federal Reserve implements that stance in modern markets with administered rates, including interest on reserve balances and the overnight reverse repurchase facility, alongside other operations. Those instruments influence short-term funding conditions, but private credit pricing depends on many layers beyond the target range.","Transmission is a chain of responses: a policy decision can influence current and expected short rates, Treasury yields, bank deposit and loan offers, exchange rates, asset valuations and spending plans. Each step has lags and uncertainty. A rate cut may coincide with a rise in long mortgage rates when expected inflation or long-run Treasury yields rise. A lender may tighten credit standards even as market rates decline. Policy actions and market outcomes should not be forced into a mechanical one-to-one formula.","Explain restrictive versus accommodative policy relative to the economy rather than by calling any positive nominal policy rate 'tight'. A policy rate of 4% when expected inflation is 5% can have a different ex ante real stance from the same 4% rate with 2% expected inflation. Financial conditions also reflect credit spreads, asset prices, dollar strength and funding liquidity. Economists make imperfect policy judgments because potential output, neutral rates and inflation expectations are uncertain.","A student should chart the policy target and an actual overnight rate separately, annotate FOMC dates, and describe a plausible transmission path to a mortgage or employer investment. This is causal reasoning, not a claim that a particular rate announcement predicts stock returns. Use Federal Reserve policy explanations rather than unverified rate-hike narratives."],"worked":["Assumed policy-rate midpoint 4.0% and expected inflation 2.5% ⇒ approximate ex ante real rate 1.5%.","At expected inflation 4.5% with unchanged nominal policy midpoint, approximate ex ante real rate −0.5%.","The two economic stances differ although the nominal policy level is the same.","A 0.25 percentage-point FOMC adjustment is not a guaranteed 0.25-point mortgage quote change.","Separate overnight policy target, Treasury yields, bank spreads and loan contract rates."],"practice":[["Why might 30-year mortgage rates rise shortly after the Federal Reserve reduces its overnight target?","Longer Treasury yields, inflation expectations, mortgage spreads and expected policy paths can rise; long mortgage quotes are not mechanically the overnight target."],["What policy instruments help anchor the overnight federal funds rate near the FOMC's target range?","Administered rates such as interest on reserve balances and the overnight reverse-repurchase facility, along with the Federal Reserve's operating framework and market operations."]]},{"title":"Term structure: what a yield curve actually compares","body":["A yield curve displays yields for comparable debt securities across maturities at a particular date. Treasury nominal securities reduce private default risk but their yields still vary with duration, inflation expectations, liquidity, term premiums and the anticipated path of overnight rates. An upward-sloping curve often means longer yields exceed short ones, but an inversion is not a statutory recession designation or a guaranteed forecast of the next twelve months.","Decompose a long yield conceptually into expected future short rates plus compensation for uncertainty, maturity, supply-demand, liquidity and market factors. That decomposition is not directly observable from a single spread; economists use models and surveys to estimate the components. A 10-year yield of 4.5% versus a 2-year yield of 4.0% gives a 0.5-percentage-point spread. Two years later that spread may change because the expected policy path or term premium changes, even without an FOMC decision.","Corporate bonds add credit spreads, which compensate for default risk, loss given default, liquidity and other risks. An investor comparing a 5.5% corporate yield with a 4.0% hypothetical comparable Treasury might describe a 1.5-point nominal credit spread, but that extra promised yield is not guaranteed extra realized return. Defaults, calls, price volatility and transaction costs matter. A high yield can be the market's price for a high chance of financial loss.","Curve analysis should compare like maturities, coupon structures, embedded options and tax treatment. A taxable corporate bond, municipal bond and Treasury are not economically equivalent simply because all display a yield. In household planning, fixed mortgage rate spreads also reflect prepayment and servicing features, so a Treasury curve alone cannot produce the lender's APR."],"worked":["Hypothetical 10-year Treasury yield 4.5%, two-year yield 4.0%.","10y−2y slope =0.5 percentage point or 50 basis points.","Hypothetical comparable corporate yield 5.5% minus Treasury 4.0% ⇒ spread 150 basis points.","A yield spread is a quoted difference, not a promise of realized incremental return.","An inverted slope can signal expectations but does not itself establish a recession."],"practice":[["Why is the Treasury 10y−2y spread not an official switch declaring recession?","It is a market-price indicator affected by expectations and premiums, while NBER downturn dating weighs broad economic activity and is determined separately."],["What risks can explain why a corporate bond yield exceeds a comparable Treasury yield?","Compensation for issuer default and recovery risk, liquidity constraints, embedded options and other security characteristics, not a free bonus."]]},{"title":"Bond cash flows, coupon versus yield and discounted value","body":["Bond value is the sum of discounted coupons and principal. Under an annual fixed-coupon toy model, P=Σ from t=1 to N of C/(1+y)^t+F/(1+y)^N. Coupon rate equals annual contractual coupon divided by face value; current yield divides coupon by current market price; yield to maturity is the discount rate that equates the observed price with contractual promised cash flows under particular timing and reinvestment-related assumptions. They are not the same quantity.","Take a $1,000 face three-year bond with a 4% annual coupon ($40 per year) and a 5% required annual yield. Present value of three $40 coupons plus $1,000 at maturity is about $972.77. The market value is below par because the contract coupon rate is less than the market yield. If the required yield falls to 3%, price rises above par. A bond at premium or discount does not change the contractual coupon dollar amount.","A zero-coupon security instead pays a single maturity amount; price P=F/(1+y)^N. For $1,000 face due in three years, annual yield of 4% gives approximately $889.00, and 5% gives about $863.84. That $25.16 price decline occurs without any principal default. Bonds sold before maturity expose owners to market price. An investor holding to maturity still has issuer, inflation, liquidity and reinvestment constraints; the terms of the bond and actual cash receipts determine outcomes.","Use effective yield consistent with payment interval. Many real US Treasury notes pay semiannual coupons, so an annual-coupon teaching formula should not be applied blindly to a live price quote. Accrued interest, clean versus dirty prices, redemption rights and bid-ask spreads matter. This module's mathematical model is educational and cannot price every market-traded debt product."],"worked":["Annual coupon bond: F=$1,000, C=$40, N=3, y=5%.","Value $40/1.05+$40/1.05²+$1,040/1.05³≈$972.77.","Zero coupon, F=$1,000, N=3, yield 4% ⇒ $888.996.","At 5% yield ⇒ $863.838, down $25.159 or about 2.83%.","Coupon rate 4%, yield 5%, and price below par are consistent."],"practice":[["Why can a bond with a 4% coupon yield 5% when bought on a secondary market?","If purchased below face value, coupon payments plus eventual par repayment generate return beyond the 4%-of-face coupon alone, subject to realized outcomes."],["What must be aligned before applying a discounting formula to a semiannual coupon security?","Convert periodic coupon cash flows, payment count and per-period discount rate consistently; account for settlement and accrued interest if pricing real securities."]]},{"title":"Duration, convexity, credit spread and interest-rate stress","body":["Duration measures timing-weighted cash-flow sensitivity to yield changes; modified duration approximates percentage price change for a small yield movement: ΔP/P≈−D_mod Δy, where Δy is a decimal yield change. This is a local linear approximation, not a maximum loss or exact forecast. A duration-six bond exposure subjected to a one-percentage-point parallel yield increase may lose approximately 6% before convexity, credit, fund flows and other effects. Interest payments and yield changes can partly offset price movement in total return over time.","Convexity improves a local price approximation for a traditional fixed bond under many non-option circumstances: ΔP/P≈−D_mod Δy+½ Convexity(Δy)². Embedded calls, prepayments and mortgage-backed security options can change effective duration and produce negative convexity. A homeowner considering refinancing is economically holding a valuable option to repay early; the lender and mortgage investors price that possibility. A simplistic 'interest rates fall so bond price goes up' rule becomes unreliable around options and credit events.","Distinguish rate duration and spread duration. A corporate credit spread can widen even when Treasury rates fall, causing the corporate price to decline. When recession fears increase, high-yield bond funds may fall because expected defaults and liquidity premiums dominate the safe-rate move. Portfolio construction must stress both components independently and together.","Evaluate a near-term down-payment fund by maximum dated cash need, not bond fund advertised yield. A long-duration fund can lose price immediately before closing. Matching short maturities, withdrawal options and acceptable credit risk can be more important than reaching for the highest forecast coupon return. Investors should read the actual fund prospectus and duration information instead of treating a fund as the same as one bond held to maturity."],"worked":["Portfolio market value $20,000, modified duration 6 years.","Parallel rate increase 0.75 percentage points ⇒ Δy=.0075.","Linear price change≈−6×.0075=−4.5%, approximate loss $900.","A simultaneous credit-spread shock can add further price decline beyond Treasury-rate effect.","Convexity and nonparallel yield-curve changes mean actual price need not equal the linear model."],"practice":[["How does modified duration differ from a bond's time remaining until maturity?","Duration weights the timing and present value of cash flows and approximates interest-rate sensitivity; coupon payments make it distinct from simple years-to-maturity."],["Why could a corporate bond fund lose value while risk-free Treasury yields fall?","Credit spreads, default expectations, liquidity or embedded option effects can worsen enough to outweigh lower government yields."]]},{"title":"Mortgage offers: contract rate, APR, points and all-in borrowing costs","body":["Consumers often treat an advertised mortgage interest rate as the full cost of a loan. The note rate determines scheduled contractual interest and amortizing principal-and-interest under the stated payment rules. Annual percentage rate (APR) under Regulation Z reflects certain finance charges in a standardized borrowing-cost calculation, so it can differ from the note rate. CFPB disclosure forms show APR, total interest percentage, closing costs, adjustable terms and projected cash to close. Neither headline percentage substitutes for actual contract and fee review.","Points paid at closing may buy a lower note rate. On a $240,000 loan, a one-point charge equals 1% or $2,400. If a lower-rate quote would reduce monthly principal and interest by $45, a simplistic undiscounted break-even is $2,400/$45≈53.3 months. The true decision also considers discounting, deduction eligibility, loan payoff or refinance timing, closing cash scarcity and whether the rate reduction lasts for the life of the loan. A borrower refinancing in two years may never recoup the extra points.","Loan amortization payment M=Lr/[1−(1+r)^(-n)], where r is periodic note rate and n payment count. For a 30-year fixed mortgage at a hypothetical 6% annual note rate, $240,000 principal produces $1,438.92 monthly P&I. At 7%, P&I is about $1,596.73, an increase around $157.81 each month, before taxes, insurance and mortgage insurance. Market quotes change and mortgage APR may include specific fees not used in this simplified P&I calculation.","Choosing a loan is a contingent household decision: ask how long the buyer expects to keep it, the accessible cash after closing, whether a rate buydown is guaranteed, prepayment provisions, private mortgage insurance and possible assistance-loan transfer restrictions. Fixed-rate mortgage monthly P&I stability is not property-tax or insurance stability. A complete comparison includes both upfront cash and monthly carrying cost."],"worked":["$240,000 loan, one discount point = $2,400 prepaid cost.","Assumed monthly P&I saving $45 ⇒ naive break-even 53.33 months, before interest and time value.","At 6% annual note, 360 months: M=240000×.005/[1−1.005^(-360)]≈$1,438.92.","At 7% annual note: M≈$1,596.73; hypothetical payment difference ≈$157.81.","Annual percentage rate and note rate are not interchangeable legal disclosures."],"practice":[["Why is a 53-month mortgage points break-even not enough to choose the offer?","It ignores the chance of early sale or refinance, opportunity cost of cash, loan term, taxes, fees, and differences in mortgage contract features."],["What distinguishes the note interest rate from APR on a Loan Estimate?","Note rate determines contractual loan interest; APR applies standardized treatment to certain finance charges and expresses a borrowing-cost rate under consumer disclosure law."]]},{"title":"Fixed, floating and inflation-indexed instruments in asset–liability matching","body":["Financial institutions and households choose between fixed and variable-rate obligations. Fixed-rate debt gives predictable contracted debt payments but can carry prepayment and early-payoff considerations. Floating-rate debt resets using a defined index plus margin, possibly with caps, floors and changes in index methodology. The borrower must read the reset interval and maximum possible payments. A variable loan can become expensive when market rates rise even if the initial monthly payment looks comfortable.","A household paying fixed P&I on a mortgage and holding cash deposits with variable interest rates has different exposure from one financing the house with an adjustable-rate mortgage and holding fixed-coupon bonds. The first may experience declining deposit yields as policy eases but stable mortgage P&I. The second could face rising debt service while its bond portfolio falls in value if rates rise. Interest-rate risk cannot be assessed by looking at assets alone: liabilities and income sensitivity must be included.","Duration matching is a concept used to reduce mismatch between assets needed and liabilities due at known times. For an expense due within twelve months, cash held in appropriate insured deposits or short-maturity high-quality securities may be more aligned than long-duration bonds. A pension paying obligations over decades has a different duration target. Matching is approximate in practice because cash-flow timing, reinvestment rates, taxes and market access vary.","For an inheritance plan, a life insurance death benefit can be conditional liquidity while an outstanding mortgage is a current obligation. The existence of a large policy face amount does not remove mortgage or policy-loan debt until cash is actually available and properly allocated. Apply the same present-value and sensitivity discipline to every type of household promise."],"worked":["Hypothetical floating-rate $100,000 debt resets from 5% to 7% annual interest.","Simple interest-only monthly cost increases from $416.67 to $583.33, +$166.67, before principal amortization.","A fixed P&I mortgage with unchanged contractual rate does not reset because short-term policy rate changes.","A bond fund of duration six can lose about 6% market price under a 1-point rate shock, approximately.","Align funding maturity to goal due date instead of assuming a quoted yield is risk-free."],"practice":[["Why can adjustable borrowing and long-duration bond investments create a double loss when rates rise?","Debt servicing can increase while bond market prices fall; both sides of the household balance sheet may be hurt by the same shock."],["Why is an individual bond held to stated maturity not equivalent to shares in a bond fund for a timed cash goal?","The individual bond has a defined maturity cash flow subject to default and terms, whereas fund shares have variable market NAV, portfolio turnover, fees and investor redemption prices."]]},{"title":"Policy shocks, scenarios and economically honest interest-rate forecasts","body":["A professional financial forecast must identify which price changes come from safe rates, which from credit or inflation risk premiums and which from product-specific terms. Federal Reserve decisions are observable; the future path and how markets respond are not. Scenarios can be conditional: short rates up 100 basis points with mortgage spreads unchanged, or long rates up 100 basis points despite a steady FOMC target, or a recession that lowers Treasuries while credit spreads widen.","Build a three-case home loan estimate with 5%, 6% and 7% hypothetical fixed note rates for the same $240,000 30-year amortization. Record principal and interest, taxes, insurance, all closing costs, points and needed reserves. On the investment side, stress a duration-six fund with −100, 0 and +100 basis-point yields, then explain that first-order duration approximations do not include coupons, convexity and credit conditions. The goal is not to pick the exact rate next spring but avoid a plan failing under plausible shocks.","Use a dated research log with FOMC releases, relevant Treasury auction data, source-specific bond pricing, and actual lender Loan Estimates when available. If no lender quote exists, label the value a hypothetical scenario instead of presenting it as current Connecticut mortgage availability. Forecasts become more defensible when they include a range, uncertainty and a decision rule.","Connect the macro story to household action: a high-rate period may reduce borrowing capacity and raise monthly payments but may also raise available short-term cash yields; a rate decline may support some asset prices but reduce deposit returns, and the direction of housing supply is uncertain. This helps the learner evaluate tradeoffs rather than attempt to time markets from headlines."],"worked":["Fictional $240,000 loan, 30 years, test note rates 5%, 6% and 7%.","Compute monthly contractual P&I independently, then add taxes/insurance and upfront closing expenses.","For duration six, a +1 percentage-point yield shock approximates −6% price; a −1 point shock approximates +6% before convexity.","Compare fixed borrowing costs and deposit interest rates as two distinct household exposures.","No rate example represents a verified live offer or forecast."],"practice":[["What makes a scenario analysis academically more responsible than an exact 'mortgage rate next year' prediction?","Explicit assumed shocks and ranges make uncertain relationships visible and test whether a plan survives plausible outcomes; no exact future rate is guaranteed."],["Which primary sources should a borrower inspect before interpreting an APR as a real finance offer?","Actual lender Loan Estimates and loan terms, CFPB disclosure explanations, and relevant source-dated market data. Hypothetical calculator defaults are not quotes."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["Federal Reserve monetary policy and mandate","https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/monetary-policy.htm"],["FOMC statements and meeting schedule","https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomc.htm"],["TreasuryDirect price and rate mechanics","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/understanding-pricing/"],["Treasury marketable securities and auction information","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/"],["CFPB Regulation Z, APR and mortgage disclosures","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1026/"],["CFPB Home loan toolkit","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"],["New York Fed reference rate / SOFR","https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/reference-rates/sofr"]],"assessment":{"title":"Rate-transmission, bond valuation and mortgage stress committee","prompt":"Present a transmission diagram linking one FOMC decision to overnight rates, longer Treasury yields, lender pricing, mortgage costs and real-world uncertainty. Price one annual-coupon and one zero-coupon bond under two yields; stress a duration-six fixed-income portfolio with yield and spread shocks; compare a $240,000 mortgage at 5%, 6% and 7% including a one-point buydown and the 53.3-month simple payback. Explain why no market quote or recession forecast can be inferred automatically.","rubric":["Accurate policy and term structure causal reasoning (20%)","Bond cash-flow prices and yield sensitivity (25%)","Mortgage APR, amortization and upfront cost comparisons (25%)","Household liability and liquidity stress (15%)","Source trace, assumptions and limitations (15%)"]}},{"id":"L7.S3","title":"The Time Value of Money","subtitle":"Discount factors, annuities, amortization, net present value, internal rates and sensitivity","prerequisites":["L1.S3 compound interest foundations","L7.S1 nominal versus real units","L7.S2 discount rates and borrowing"],"objectives":["Derive compound and present-value growth factors","Convert periodic rates accurately and distinguish APY from APR","Price level annuity, growing annuity and perpetuity cash flows","Derive amortizing loan payments and remaining balances","Interpret project NPV, IRR and multiple-IRR pitfalls","Evaluate rent versus own and life-insurance funding with timing-aware cash flows"],"sections":[{"title":"Why financial decisions must bring money to a common date","body":["A dollar received today and a dollar promised in ten years are not interchangeable. Cash available now may pay a bill, remain liquid or fund an investment; promised future cash can be risky, delayed and worth less in purchasing power. The time value of money expresses these differences using cash-flow dates and a discount rate. Future value answers what an amount becomes under an assumed accumulation rate; present value answers what a specified later payment is worth in today's units under an assumed opportunity cost and risk. Neither formula supplies a guaranteed return.","The compound equation is FV=PV(1+r)^n for a constant effective periodic rate r and n periods. Rearranging, PV=FV/(1+r)^n. If $1,000 grows at a hypothetical effective 6% a year for five years, its future value is about $1,338.23. If a payer owes $1,338.23 in five years and the correct model discount rate is 6%, present value is about $1,000. The formula is reversible because dates and per-period rate were treated consistently.","Compounding captures an exponential process: each period's interest or return can itself earn a return. The number of compounding periods matters, as does the rate's periodic unit. A project claiming 6% 'per month' cannot be entered as annual 6% without conversion. Negative returns are also possible, particularly for investments. A sequence of realized returns should be multiplied as growth factors rather than replaced by a smooth forecast.","Start each model with a time line showing t=0, cash paid or received at each date, whether payments occur at beginning or end of periods, and the uncertainty of each payment. State whether the number is a nominal cash amount or inflation-adjusted real amount. Many costly errors result from correct algebra applied to wrong dates or currencies."],"worked":["PV $1,000, effective annual r=6%, n=5 ⇒ FV=$1,000×1.06^5≈$1,338.23.","Discount that payment five periods at 6% ⇒ $1,338.23/1.06^5≈$1,000.","At negative 10% then positive 10%, growth factor .90×1.10=.99 ⇒ 1% loss.","A cash flow dated today does not receive a year's discount merely because a spreadsheet has annual columns.","All rates and periods must use the same time unit."],"practice":[["Why does the discount rate represent more than a mathematical preference?","It reflects opportunity cost, time and risk assumptions relevant to the cash flow and can incorporate inflation consistently; different choices change value."],["Why is '6% annual rate' inadequate without specifying nominal versus effective compounding conventions?","Monthly and annual compounding produce different effective growth factors at the same nominal quoted rate; timing and conversion must be defined."]]},{"title":"Annual percentage yields, effective rates and compounding frequency","body":["Advertised nominal annual rates and effective annual yields are not automatically the same. If a nominal annual rate j compounds m times per year, effective annual rate is (1+j/m)^m−1. A nominal 6% annual rate compounded monthly yields (1+.06/12)^12−1≈6.1678%. If interest compounds annually at 6%, the effective annual yield is exactly 6%. For comparisons, bring products to the same effective annual convention and then account for taxes, fees, withdrawal limits and credit risk.","Bank deposit annual percentage yield (APY) is a yield disclosure that incorporates compounding assumptions for deposits. Consumer credit APR is a different regulated disclosure measure that can reflect certain finance charges and credit rules; a mortgage APR should not be treated as an APY on a savings account or as the note rate used in monthly P&I. APR, APY and investment expected return answer different questions and should not be placed into one comparison table without descriptions of what is included.","For a constant effective annual rate i, the equivalent monthly rate is r=(1+i)^(1/12)−1. If i=6%, r≈0.4868% per month, not exactly 0.5%. Conversely, if a contract states nominal 6% with monthly compounding, the applicable periodic rate may be 0.5%. These distinctions accumulate over decades, particularly in amortization and retirement projections.","Always test boundary conditions: zero rate, short period, long horizon and an expense that changes over time. A model returning undefined values at zero rate is a programming bug, not a financial conclusion. Avoid displaying too many decimal places, because future rates and taxes are more uncertain than the computer's arithmetic precision."],"worked":["Nominal 6% annual rate, monthly compounding: effective rate (1+.06/12)^12−1≈6.1678%.","Effective 6% annual rate converted to monthly: (1.06)^(1/12)−1≈0.4868%.","An account with nominal 6% compounded annually earns effective 6%, not 6.1678%.","Rate comparison should include accessibility, account protection, tax and fees, not APY alone.","The zero-rate case uses simple equal-period sums, not division by zero."],"practice":[["What is the difference between nominal 6% with monthly compounding and 6% effective annual return?","The monthly-compounded nominal rate generates effective annual growth of about 6.1678%, while a 6% effective annual return generates approximately 0.4868% per month."],["Why does APR on a mortgage not directly equal the note rate that determines the contractual P&I payment?","APR is a regulated credit-cost calculation incorporating specified finance charges and timing, while note rate describes interest applied to the contract's outstanding principal."]]},{"title":"Level annuities, beginning-of-period payments and future goals","body":["An ordinary annuity makes equal payments at each period's end. At periodic constant r and n payments, FV=C[(1+r)^n−1]/r, with FV=Cn when r=0. For a target balance FV at the end of n periods, rearrange to find contribution C=FV r/[(1+r)^n−1]. This assumes consistent payments, no missed contributions and a deterministic constant return. For investments, a smooth annual return is illustrative rather than a forecast.","An annuity due pays each amount at the beginning of the period. Relative to an ordinary annuity with otherwise identical terms, future value is multiplied by (1+r), because each contribution compounds for one additional period. This matters for deposits made on each payday versus month-end and for first-of-month rent payments. Beginners often compute 12 deposits and accidentally count 13 periods, exaggerating wealth.","With $250 end-of-month deposits for 240 months and a 6% nominal annual rate compounded monthly, future value is $250[(1+.06/12)^240−1]/(.06/12)≈$115,510.22, of which $60,000 represents contributions. That is before fees, taxes and inflation. If the same amounts were deposited at each month start, multiply by 1.005, producing about $116,087.77. The difference comes solely from timing, not an investment strategy or higher assumed rate.","To fund a ten-year home purchase, do not automatically assume a 6% risk asset return if the purchase cannot tolerate a late loss. Instead test a low or zero return and compute the required monthly saving. A household with a $12,000 cash target and 24 month-end deposits needs $500/month before interest. Tax-advantaged retirement money cannot always be treated as unrestricted home cash."],"worked":["C=$250/month, r=.06/12=.005, n=240 ⇒ ordinary FV≈$115,510.22.","Total deposits $250×240=$60,000; hypothetical growth ≈$55,510.22.","Annuity-due FV=$115,510.22×1.005≈$116,087.77.","To accumulate $12,000 in 24 deposits with r=0: $12,000/24=$500/month.","No smooth-return annuity equation captures real-world investment losses or tax effects."],"practice":[["Why is a $250 monthly deposit at the beginning of the month worth more in the annuity model than at month-end?","Each deposit has one extra compounding period; the assumed per-period effective return factor multiplies ordinary-annuity future value."],["How do you compute the required monthly contribution to accumulate $15,000 in 30 months if return is zero?","Divide target by payments: $15,000/30=$500 per month, before costs and any changes to the target."]]},{"title":"Present value of obligations, growing payments and perpetuities","body":["Present value of a level ordinary annuity is PV=C[1−(1+r)^(-n)]/r, with PV=Cn when r=0. This converts a series of dated payments into a common present amount. If a family requires $20,000 annual income replacement at each year end for ten years and discount rate is hypothetically 4%, PV≈$162,217.92. That value may help frame life-insurance needs, but the correct discount rate, survivor taxes, mortality timing and available benefits must be considered before recommending actual coverage.","Growing annuities model payments rising at g per period. For first payment C1 at t=1, a level-growth ordinary annuity value is PV=C1[1−((1+g)/(1+r))^n]/(r−g), for r different from g, subject to economic model consistency. If r=g, the limit is PV=n C1/(1+r). A constant-growth perpetuity is C1/(r−g) only when r>g and an indefinitely growing payout is sustainable. An absurd valuation when r≈g signals sensitivity to assumptions, not an actual infinite asset value.","Suppose a hypothetical permanent spending obligation begins at $10,000 one year from now and grows 2% each year, discounted at 5%. The simplistic perpetuity value is $10,000/(.05−.02)=$333,333.33. If required return increases to 6% while g stays 2%, value falls to $250,000. A small rate change can substantially alter the calculated value of long-lasting claims; real human spending obligations are finite and uncertain.","Inflation and discount rates must share nominal or real units. If expenses rise in nominal dollars, use a nominal discount model. Applying a nominal growth rate to real-dollar spending and discounting at a real rate mixes units and exaggerates costs. Asset protection and legacy planning require clarity about whether dollar amounts represent today's purchasing power."],"worked":["Level cash flow $20,000, years 10, r=4% ⇒ PV=$20,000[1−1.04^(−10)]/.04≈$162,217.92.","Perpetual first-year cash $10,000, r=5%, g=2% ⇒ PV≈$333,333.33.","At r=6%, g=2%, same cash ⇒ PV=$250,000.","When r=g, finite growing-annuity PV has a stable limiting formula, not a singular financial event.","Nominal growth and nominal discount belong together; real and real belong together."],"practice":[["Why is a constant-growth perpetuity formula invalid when the growth rate equals or exceeds the discount rate?","The infinite sum fails to converge, so the formula does not produce a finite economically meaningful valuation without revising assumptions or horizon."],["Why should inflation-adjusted benefit needs and nominal investment discount rates not be mixed without conversion?","They are different purchasing-power units; mismatching rates to cash-flow types distorts the present value of long-term obligations."]]},{"title":"Mortgage amortization: payment split, remaining debt and extra principal","body":["An amortizing loan requires a constant payment that covers periodic interest and reduces principal under the assumed terms. For original balance L, periodic rate r and n payments, M=Lr/[1−(1+r)^(-n)], or M=L/n at zero rate. At each month t, interest I_t=r B_(t−1), principal reduction P_t=M−I_t and new balance B_t=B_(t−1)−P_t. Initially most of a long mortgage's payment can represent interest, because the outstanding principal is high.","At an illustrative 30-year fixed 6% nominal mortgage rate on $240,000, monthly P&I is about $1,438.92. First month's interest is $240,000×.005=$1,200 and principal reduction about $238.92. Payment number 2 accrues interest on the smaller balance, so the principal share rises slightly. An amortization table is not proof the entire housing bill equals $1,438.92: property taxes, insurance, PMI, maintenance and utilities remain separate.","The remaining balance after k fixed payments can be computed as B_k=L(1+r)^k−M[(1+r)^k−1]/r, assuming the scheduled payment, rate and terms remain unchanged. Extra principal payments reduce the outstanding balance, potentially the total interest cost and time to payoff if the lender applies them to principal. The exact benefit varies with timing, loan servicer processing, prepayment rules, escrow and whether the loan is recast or refinanced.","A household saving for a down payment should compare extra mortgage principal with reserves and other debt. Paying an additional $2,000 toward the loan might save future interest but remove cash needed for a roof repair. The correct financial decision weighs contractual debt cost, after-tax investment alternatives, liquidity and household risk capacity."],"worked":["Loan $240,000, APR-like note input 6% nominal annual, 360 months ⇒ M≈$1,438.92 P&I.","First month interest $240,000×.005=$1,200.","First month principal paid ≈$238.92; next balance≈$239,761.08.","Remaining balance formula makes prepayment timing explicit.","This P&I model excludes all property and insurance costs."],"practice":[["What determines first-month interest in a standard fixed-rate amortizing mortgage?","The stated periodic interest rate multiplied by the beginning outstanding principal, not the original term times the rate or the full payment amount."],["Can a mortgage be affordable based on P&I while the actual monthly housing costs remain unaffordable?","Yes. Taxes, insurance, mortgage insurance, repairs, fees and other expenses must be included in a cash-flow and reserve analysis."]]},{"title":"NPV decision rules and why IRR can mislead","body":["Net present value (NPV) places all incremental cash flows at the same valuation date: NPV=Σ CF_t/(1+r)^t, including the initial investment at t=0. A positive NPV under a defensible required return suggests an investment's model value exceeds its economic outlay. The discount rate should reflect opportunity cost and the risk of the modeled flows, and the cash flows must be incremental and after appropriate taxes when evaluating household wealth. A positive spreadsheet number is a conditional conclusion, not a guarantee that the project pays obligations when due.","Internal rate of return (IRR) is a discount rate for which NPV equals zero. For a simple conventional cash flow with one initial outflow and positive later receipts, an IRR may be intuitive. With cash-flow sign changes, multiple IRRs or no real economically useful IRR may exist. IRR comparisons can rank differently from NPV when projects differ in size or timing; reinvestment assumptions and risk vary. Professional investment evaluation generally prioritizes NPV at the appropriate cost of capital under a coherent capital allocation.","Consider an illustrative investment costing $1,000 now that pays $600 at the end of each of two years. At a 10% effective annual discount rate, NPV=−$1,000+$600/1.1+$600/1.1²≈$41.32. A different opportunity paying $2,000 in one year for a $1,500 investment would have NPV≈$318.18 at 10%, if its stated outcome were credible. Comparing those model NPVs requires evaluating certainty, liquidity, risk and capital constraints rather than mechanically choosing the percentage IRR.","Risk scenarios need real cash-flow dates: a house purchase may involve immediate closing costs, ongoing maintenance, selling expenses and uncertain future prices; insurance contracts have premiums, cash surrender values and potential loan balances; retirement contributions have tax consequences. Treating one future asset value as pure profit while omitting cash costs is a valuation error. Document what information would invalidate the project."],"worked":["Project outlay $1,000 at t=0; receipts $600 at t=1 and $600 at t=2.","Discount rate 10% ⇒ NPV=−1000+600/1.1+600/1.1²≈$41.32.","An alternative outlay $1,500 and return $2,000 next year ⇒ NPV≈$318.18 at 10%.","Both projects may appear attractive but cash-flow risk and liquidity can invalidate a decision.","IRR is not a substitute for checking project size and sign changes."],"practice":[["Why can a project with higher percentage IRR still have lower economic value creation?","Project scale, cash-flow dates and risk can differ; NPV at a relevant required return measures dollar value and can rank opportunities differently."],["Why is an investment with model-positive NPV unsuitable for money due next week?","A distant uncertain payment does not provide immediate liquid cash. The household may fail required obligations despite conditional model value."]]},{"title":"Loan refinancing and insurance-contract valuation as dated alternatives","body":["Refinancing is an upfront-cost-versus-future-payment decision. A lower interest rate may reduce P&I and total future interest, but new origination fees, points, appraisal, title fees and any change in remaining term can outweigh the benefit. A practical first screen is simple payback: incremental closing cash divided by monthly saving, where monthly saving is positive and comparable. A more rigorous model discounts the expected incremental payments over the actual horizon and includes the remaining balance at sale, prepayment and tax differences.","Suppose refinance costs $4,000 now and saves a fixed hypothetical $90 per month for 60 months until a likely sale. Simple undiscounted payback is 44.44 months, and total undiscounted savings are $5,400. At a monthly discount rate of 0.5%, the PV of 60 month-end savings is $90[1−1.005^(−60)]/.005≈$4,655. A simple NPV of savings less upfront cost is about $655, before remaining mortgage balance differences, lender terms, taxes and opportunity cost. If the sale arrives at month 24, the project may have negative NPV.","A permanent life insurance purchase can also be modeled as a series of conditional cash flows: premiums paid, guaranteed and non-guaranteed surrender values, debt accrued on policy loans, death-benefit contingencies and possible tax consequences. But a simplified financial annuity cannot price mortality protection without assumptions about survival and actual product rights. Comparing $250 premium with $30 term coverage must include different death-benefit risks, contract duration, investment returns and cash availability.","Finish each comparison with at least one sensitivity on discount rate, timing and tax or fee changes. A professionally documented recommendation identifies what would need to be verified in an actual Loan Estimate or insurer in-force illustration and separates mathematical illustration from individualized advice. This prevents the misleading conclusion that compounding itself creates wealth independent of capital, risk and legally enforceable obligations."],"worked":["Hypothetical refinance cost $4,000, monthly saving $90 for 60 months.","Simple payback $4,000/$90≈44.44 months.","Monthly discount rate .5% ⇒ PV savings≈$90[1−1.005^(−60)]/.005.","PV of savings approximately $4,655.29, so NPV after cost approximately $655.29.","A 24-month horizon changes the economics and must include remaining balance effects."],"practice":[["Why isn't a refinance's total expected 60-month payment saving its exact economic benefit?","Savings occur over time and may disappear if the loan ends early; upfront costs, discounting, remaining loan balances and taxes change true NPV."],["Why can't the annuity equation alone establish whether a cash-value life policy is a good investment?","Life policies combine contingent death protection, insurer crediting, contract charges, surrender values, loan terms and tax rights; a simple smooth-return cash model omits material terms."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFPB Mortgage Loan Estimate and Closing Disclosure","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"],["CFPB Understanding APR and mortgage cost disclosures","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1026/"],["Investor.gov compound-interest education","https://www.investor.gov/financial-tools-calculators/calculators/compound-interest-calculator"],["TreasuryDirect prices and discounting","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/understanding-pricing/"],["CFA Institute quantitative methods learning resources","https://www.cfainstitute.org/programs/cfa-program/candidate-resources/level-i-exam"]],"assessment":{"title":"Financial mathematics cumulative problem set","prompt":"Derive PV/FV and monthly rate conversions. Compute future values of an ordinary annuity and annuity due, a ten-year present value of survivor expenses, a finite growing annuity, and a 30-year $240,000 mortgage amortization under 6% and 7% notes. Compute NPV for one two-payment investment and a refinance with upfront fee, including a 24- versus 60-month hold and sensitivities. Defend the chosen discount rate, real versus nominal units and household liquidity minimums.","rubric":["Compounding and rate conventions (20%)","Annuity and PV derivations (25%)","Loan amortization accuracy and alternative timing (25%)","NPV and IRR interpretation, errors and risk (20%)","Documentation, citations and limitations (10%)"]}},{"id":"L7.S4","title":"Economic Indicators and Cycles","subtitle":"GDP, labor statistics, inflation data, recession dating, leading indicators and scenario research","prerequisites":["L7.S1 inflation indexes and real units","L7.S2 rates and the yield curve","L7.S3 growth and discounting"],"objectives":["Compute GDP contributions and nominal-versus-real growth","Differentiate employment, unemployment, participation and wage measures","Interpret CPI versus PCE inflation with methodological caution","Distinguish NBER business-cycle dating from media rules of thumb","Evaluate leading, coincident and lagging economic indicators","Avoid look-ahead bias by using vintages and documented revisions","Connect economic conditions to investment, wage and mortgage risks"],"sections":[{"title":"Economic indicators are measurements with definitions and release dates","body":["Economic statistics measure aspects of a large and changing system. A GDP estimate, an employment report, a price index and consumer sentiment survey answer different questions; none is a complete scorecard for the household's welfare. Every series has a reference period, definition, sampling or data-compilation method, publication time, units, revision policy and seasonal adjustment. A numerically precise chart can still mislead when those specifications are hidden.","The Bureau of Economic Analysis estimates GDP using multiple data sources and publishes advance, second and third estimates for a quarter as more information arrives. CPI and labor statistics use Bureau of Labor Statistics methods; the Federal Reserve Economic Data (FRED) platform republishes many series with identifiers and source metadata. In historical research, identify what data was actually available at the decision date rather than using a future-revised number to claim a forecast would have worked.","An indicator can be quoted as a level, monthly change, quarterly change at annual rate, or year-over-year change. A quarterly real GDP increase of 0.5% is not the same statement as a 0.5% annualized rate; compounded at four quarters, a 0.5% quarterly change becomes about 2.015% annualized. Headline GDP and inflation release narratives often mix those units. Students should annotate them on every graph.","A research memo must state an observation date, release date, series definition and revision vintage. For example, if a job report is published after an investor made a mortgage decision, it cannot be used as evidence that the investor knew the future job count. Clean separation between observations, forecasts and interpretations is a baseline standard for academic financial analysis."],"worked":["Hypothetical quarterly real GDP factor 1.005 (0.5% growth).","Annualized rate (1.005)^4−1≈2.015%, not 0.5%.","A quarterly level reading and annualized growth rate represent different units.","Advance GDP estimate may be revised as more source data arrives.","Report what vintage was available at a decision date."],"practice":[["Why can a backtested forecast look artificially accurate if it uses today's revised historical GDP series?","Later information was not known to decision-makers at the earlier time. Using revised future data introduces look-ahead bias and invalidates a true real-time test."],["What distinction should a chart make between quarterly GDP growth and annualized quarterly GDP growth?","A one-quarter rate measures growth across the quarter; annualization compounds that quarterly pace as if repeated for four quarters, a hypothetical rate not a realized full year."]]},{"title":"GDP accounting, output, nominal growth and real volume","body":["Gross domestic product measures the market value of final goods and services produced within a geographic economy during a defined period. The familiar expenditure identity is Y=C+I+G+(X−M), where C denotes personal consumption, I gross private domestic investment, G government consumption and investment, X exports and M imports. Intermediate goods are excluded to reduce double counting. GDP does not add up the market value of existing homes merely because titles change, though qualifying brokerage and construction-related new services are part of current economic output.","Nominal GDP uses current period prices and quantities; real GDP estimates production while adjusting for price changes, using chain-type measures in US national accounts. One cannot simply divide every modern BEA chained-dollar component by a common price index and expect exact additivity of displayed component levels. For simple illustration, if nominal output increases 6% while the overall GDP price level rises 3%, an approximate multiplicative real-growth estimate is 1.06/1.03−1≈2.91%. It is a teaching identity using one price factor, not an exact reconstruction of BEA's chain methods.","Consider a fictional economy with $700 billion consumer spending, $180 billion private investment, $210 billion government purchases, $90 billion exports and $120 billion imports. GDP by expenditure is $700+$180+$210+$90−$120=$1,060 billion. Imports are subtracted because some spending components contain foreign-produced goods; this is a national-accounting adjustment, not proof imports 'hurt' GDP dollar for dollar as a causal statement. A decline in imports can coincide with weak household demand.","Real output growth can occur while real wages are stagnant or local employment declines. GDP is a production aggregate, not a measure of distribution, ecological health, household wealth or economic security. Financial planning should combine output with labor and price data and never infer that the typical household's budget improved just because quarterly GDP was positive."],"worked":["C=$700b, I=$180b, G=$210b, X=$90b, imports M=$120b.","Expenditure GDP =700+180+210+(90−120)=$1,060b.","Nominal output growth 6%, illustrative price-level growth 3% ⇒ real 1.06/1.03−1≈2.9126%.","This ratio is a conceptual example; BEA real GDP uses chain-type accounting.","GDP does not measure median household disposable income or net worth."],"practice":[["Why does the expenditure identity subtract imports from domestic consumption and investment totals?","Domestic spending aggregates include some imported items; subtracting imports removes foreign output so GDP measures domestic production."],["What limitations prevent GDP alone from describing a household's financial well-being?","GDP lacks detail about distribution, tax burdens, living costs, job security, household debt, leisure and individual access to the resulting output."]]},{"title":"Labor markets: unemployment rate, labor force and hidden slack","body":["The headline U-3 unemployment rate measures unemployed people actively seeking and available for work, subject to the survey definitions, divided by the civilian labor force. The labor force is employed plus officially unemployed people; someone not seeking work can be outside the labor force rather than counted as unemployed. The employment-to-population ratio and labor-force participation measure other dimensions of labor markets. Broader U-6 underutilization includes additional labor-market categories such as certain marginally attached workers and part-time employees working part time for economic reasons.","Assume a fictional labor market with 950 people employed, 50 actively unemployed and seeking work, and 200 outside the labor force. Labor force is 1,000; unemployment is 50/1,000=5%; participation is 1,000/1,200≈83.33%; employment-to-population is 950/1,200≈79.17%. If 20 unemployed people stop looking and move outside the labor force while employment stays 950, U-3 can fall to 30/980≈3.06% even though no new jobs were created. That is why falling unemployment does not always signify an improving labor market.","Payroll establishment surveys and household surveys estimate different dimensions. Payroll employment counts jobs, while household employment counts people under its definitions; one person can hold more than one payroll job. Revisions, seasonal factors, immigration, demographics and participation affect interpretations. Wage growth can be nominal while purchasing power declines when inflation is higher; combine real wage data with hours and benefit costs rather than quote just a headline hourly increase.","Household risk analysis should incorporate the possibility of wage interruption. An area with rising layoffs may warrant a larger cash runway before a home purchase, even if long-run average GDP is growing. Macro labor data can help identify scenarios, but a broad unemployment rate is not a guaranteed probability that a specific worker will lose a job."],"worked":["Employed 950; unemployed seeking 50; outside labor force 200.","Labor force 1,000; unemployment 50/1,000=5%.","Working-age population in model 1,200; participation 1,000/1,200≈83.33%.","After 20 unemployed stop seeking, U-3 becomes 30/980≈3.06% without job creation.","Unemployment and labor-force participation must be read together."],"practice":[["Why can headline unemployment decline when no additional people find work?","Some job seekers may stop searching and leave the counted labor force, lowering both numerator and denominator; employment can remain unchanged."],["Why shouldn't payroll jobs and household employed-person counts be used as interchangeable series?","One measures payroll positions and the other surveyed people's employment status; multiple-job holding and methodology make their counts differ."]]},{"title":"CPI, PCE and prices: differing baskets and monetary-policy interpretation","body":["Price indexes vary by population, coverage, weights and methods. BLS CPI measures price changes for specified consumer baskets and populations. The Bureau of Economic Analysis personal consumption expenditures (PCE) price index covers a different consumption scope and uses chain-type index approaches. The Federal Reserve emphasizes PCE inflation as a central reference for its long-run price-stability objective. Their rates may diverge in a particular period without one necessarily being wrong.","Core inflation measures generally remove food and energy price categories to help distinguish persistent broad price trends from some volatile items; those omitted expenses still matter greatly to households. Headline inflation affects the overall basket, while core can be analytically helpful for policy. When gasoline prices fall and rents rise, different indexes and groups may experience different inflation. A policy forecast should not dismiss the family's food or electricity bills simply because a core measure excludes them.","Suppose a price index rises from 300 to 306 in a year; year-over-year inflation is 2%. If the previous year had surged 10%, slowing to 2% is disinflation. The overall price level remains above its earlier value. Inflation easing does not imply home sale prices will decline, or that a future mortgage rate is mechanically lower. Expectations, credit spreads, economic growth and collateral risk also matter.","A student economic dashboard should display CPI-U headline, a relevant core series, PCE inflation and an explicitly modeled household inflation measure. Each requires clear unit and observation-date labels. The purpose is not to argue that one index represents everyone's experience, but to understand which question each statistic answers and which real cash flow decisions it informs."],"worked":["Illustrative price index 300 to 306 ⇒ 2% annual inflation.","Prior year 10%, next year 2% ⇒ disinflation while prices continue rising.","An individual household spending disproportionately on housing may experience different inflation from CPI-U or PCE.","Core inflation excludes volatile categories analytically; it does not exclude them from the family's budget.","Separate policy interpretation of price statistics from direct household spending needs."],"practice":[["Why can PCE inflation and CPI inflation differ in the same month?","Their coverage, basket weights, data sources and index calculation methods differ; both are constructed measures with different purposes."],["Why is a lower core inflation reading not proof the household's living costs are declining?","Core rates can remain positive and exclude food and energy price changes that still affect actual family expenses."]]},{"title":"Business cycles, NBER recession dating and recession myths","body":["The US National Bureau of Economic Research Business Cycle Dating Committee identifies peaks and troughs based on broad economic activity. Its conventional recession interpretation concerns a significant decline spread across the economy lasting more than a few months, evaluated with multiple indicators. Two consecutive negative quarterly real GDP readings are a commonly repeated heuristic, not the official US recession designation. The committee can identify a recession even when the simple two-quarter rule fails, and dating can happen after the fact.","Business-cycle phases are expansions between trough and peak, and contractions from peak to trough. The return to a prior peak is not necessarily a return to the prior long-run growth trend, household wage quality or local housing market condition. A short downturn can cause substantial lasting financial damage to particular families; different industries and regions respond differently.","Leading indicators are intended to move before broader economic conditions under certain historical relationships; coincident indicators describe current activity; lagging indicators respond after conditions change. The yield curve and some surveys are forward-looking, but their predictive power changes with policies, financial structures and market expectations. An indicator that preceded past recessions is not a deterministic clock. Analysts should use conditional odds, source dates and counterevidence rather than binary declarations.","Build a recession stress test for financial planning: job-loss duration, a 25% equity market drawdown, house-sale price decline, higher credit spreads and delays in selling illiquid assets. Not all shocks must occur simultaneously, but correlations can strengthen in stress. A household with an emergency reserve and affordable mortgage can be more resilient than one whose investment forecast relies on uninterrupted income and forced asset sales."],"worked":["Assume two quarterly real GDP changes of −0.3% and −0.2%.","Compounded six-month factor .997×.998≈.995006 ⇒ total ≈−0.4994%.","Two quarters of decline are a recession rule of thumb, not an official NBER declaration.","In risk scenario, $20,000 equities falling 25% become $15,000 before fees and taxes.","A model must distinguish recession dating from market and household stress."],"practice":[["Who officially dates US business-cycle peaks and troughs for the standard NBER chronology?","The National Bureau of Economic Research's Business Cycle Dating Committee, which evaluates broad activity measures rather than applying a fixed two-negative-quarters rule."],["Why is a yield-curve inversion not sufficient evidence to assert a recession will begin exactly next quarter?","The spread reflects changing rates, premiums and expectations; predictive relationships are imperfect and recession timing is uncertain."]]},{"title":"Leading and lagging signals, revisions, and research without look-ahead bias","body":["Economic forecasting requires choosing what to measure and what was known at the time. Leading candidates include selected new orders, housing starts or permits, credit spreads, surveys and yield curve shapes. Lagging or coincident candidates include payroll employment, industrial production and other activity data, though classifications vary. In a given episode, real estate supply restrictions can make housing starts diverge from prices, while an inflation shock can cause equity and bonds to fall together. No set of indicators guarantees a market timing profit.","Data vintages are the original values available on historical release dates. Government series frequently undergo annual revisions, seasonal re-estimation or methodological changes; financial researchers can consult the Federal Reserve's ALFRED vintage database alongside FRED. If a forecasting regression uses final revised GDP values and calls them the information available before the recession, it commits look-ahead bias. A defensible study freezes the dataset at each forecast origin and evaluates out-of-sample results with explicit revisions and confidence intervals.","Different growth rates need proper calculation. A level going from 100 to 103 one month and then 103 to 104 the next grows 3% in the first month and approximately 0.9709% in the next, not 1%. Annualized rates compound rather than simply multiply for exact values. A 0.5% quarterly rate corresponds to (1.005)^4−1≈2.015% annualized, not an observed year of 2.015% growth.","A formal memo can separate evidence into data, interpretation and proposed action: the data statement is 'credit spreads widened by 100 basis points' in a hypothetical scenario; interpretation is that investors may demand more credit compensation; action is to stress borrowing costs and cash reserves. Only the first is a measured observation if grounded in sourced data. Keep scenario assumptions visibly labeled."],"worked":["Hypothetical index 100→103: first month growth 3%.","103→104: second month growth 104/103−1≈0.9709%.","Quarterly growth 0.5% repeated four times ⇒ (1.005)^4−1≈2.015%.","If an original GDP estimate is later revised, a backtest must not substitute the later value at earlier decisions.","Maintain forecast date, release date, series ID and vintage."],"practice":[["What is data-vintage leakage and why does it bias economic forecasts?","It occurs when a historical backtest incorporates revised or newly published data unavailable at the earlier decision date, making the forecast appear more knowledgeable than it was."],["Why is annualizing one quarter's growth not the same as measuring a full year of GDP growth?","Annualization assumes that quarterly rate repeats four times; actual subsequent quarters can have different growth or contraction."]]},{"title":"Economics to household decisions: jobs, markets, home prices and income shocks","body":["Macro policy can influence household balance sheets without dictating any one person's outcome. When inflation rises, cash may lose real buying power, interest rates may adjust and living costs can increase faster than wages. When growth slows, wages or hours may fall, loan standards can tighten, asset values can drop and employers can suspend hiring. A family preparing to buy a home must test access to credit, total payment, property taxes, insurance, and continued ability to work under multiple economic states.","Suppose a household's essential monthly expenses are $3,000 and it keeps $12,000 accessible emergency savings. Its initial simple cash runway is four months in the no-income, no-benefit scenario. If monthly essentials climb 10% to $3,300, the same $12,000 covers about 3.64 months. If an extra $2,500 deductible or repair is due at the same time, available reserve falls to $9,500, which funds only 2.88 months. This illustrates correlation between economic stress and household unexpected spending.","House price sensitivity is local and leveraged. A nominal house value of $250,000 with $237,500 mortgage has $12,500 gross equity before selling costs. A 10% house-price decline eliminates that equity and yields a negative $12,500 nominal amount before sale costs, assuming unchanged loan balance. Broad GDP growth cannot guarantee positive property equity in a particular street or neighborhood. Research actual local comparable sales, lender constraints and insurer terms before a purchase.","Conclude the economics module with a risk-aware action framework: protect essential cash obligations; keep a separate dated home fund; select investments appropriate to horizon and capacity; monitor real salary, housing costs and loan terms; and revise assumptions when credible data changes. An indicator dashboard supports choices but does not replace individual budgeting. Its ethical purpose is to improve decisions rather than generate confident predictions of uncontrollable markets."],"worked":["Essential costs $3,000/month and cash $12,000 ⇒ runway 4.00 months.","10% expense increase ⇒ $3,300/month and runway 12,000/3,300≈3.64 months.","After $2,500 unexpected deductible ⇒ reserve $9,500 and runway 9,500/3,300≈2.88 months.","$250,000 home with $237,500 debt loses $25,000 market value at −10%, leaving negative $12,500 gross equity.","All values are illustrative, without actual job, tax or mortgage forecasts."],"practice":[["Why is a four-month reserve not guaranteed to cover four months after inflation and a property repair?","The reserve is nominal while expenses can rise or a large deductible can consume cash immediately, reducing months of runway."],["Why is it inappropriate to infer a Connecticut home's price will increase because national GDP grew this quarter?","Home values depend on local supply, demand, interest rates, taxes, condition and financing; national GDP is neither a precise local price predictor nor an equity guarantee."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["BEA real and nominal GDP, revisions and measurement","https://www.bea.gov/resources/learning-center/what-to-know-gdp"],["BEA recession definition and NBER distinction","https://www.bea.gov/help/glossary/recession"],["NBER Business Cycle Dating Committee","https://www.nber.org/research/business-cycle-dating"],["BLS Employment Situation","https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.toc.htm"],["BLS Labor Force Statistics glossary","https://www.bls.gov/bls/glossary.htm"],["BLS CPI methodology","https://www.bls.gov/cpi/"],["BEA PCE inflation","https://www.bea.gov/data/personal-consumption-expenditures-price-index"],["Federal Reserve ALFRED data vintages","https://fred.stlouisfed.org/"],["Federal Reserve monetary-policy transmission","https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/monetary-policy.htm"]],"assessment":{"title":"Real-time US economic data and Connecticut household committee","prompt":"Produce a four-indicator economic briefing with annotated source/release dates, construct nominal versus real GDP growth, compute unemployment and participation using a consistent fictional labor force, separate CPI from PCE and disinflation from deflation, evaluate a hypothetical yield-curve inversion without declaring a certain recession, and run an emergency cash/housing-equity stress under a job-loss, inflation and house-price shock. Explain data revisions and no-look-ahead forecasting.","rubric":["GDP and inflation math with correctly labeled periods (20%)","Labor market statistics and interpretation (20%)","NBER dating and indicator causal caution (20%)","Data vintage methodology and counterevidence (20%)","Household stress, source trace and justified decisions (20%)"]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Connecticut Home, Retirement and Business-Cycle Investment Committee","intro":"An interdisciplinary college-style assessment linking Module 7's four chapters. Use the fictional assumptions; do not substitute live mortgage rates, actual property prices, current economic forecasts, or a guaranteed investment result. Show every equation, decision date and unit.","scenario":["A Connecticut household earns $50,000 annually and believes it can save $250 each month once essentials are covered. The family hopes to purchase a $250,000 home in the medium term and provide long-horizon support for a daughter. Under one financing illustration, the mortgage starts at $237,500, meaning only $12,500 gross equity before closing costs.","National CPI is hypothetically 300 at t=0 and 312 after one year; next year it increases another 3%. Household spending weights are 45% housing, 20% groceries, 15% transport, 10% utilities and 10% other, with annual price shocks +6%, +3%, −2%, +8% and +4%, respectively.","A nominal savings account earns 2% and national inflation is assumed 5% in the stress year. A long-horizon investment illustration earns 6% nominal with monthly compounding, but actual returns could be negative. All comparisons must be made in consistent purchasing-power units.","Fictional Treasury 10-year nominal yield is 4.5% and comparable maturity TIPS real yield is 2%. Another $1,000-face zero-coupon Treasury is due in 3 years and market yield changes from 4% to 5%. A duration-six $20,000 bond exposure is shocked by an additional +0.75 percentage-point yield increase.","A potential $240,000 30-year mortgage is evaluated at hypothetical note rates of 5%, 6% and 7%, with P&I calculated from standard monthly amortization. One-point upfront buydown costs $2,400 and is claimed to save $45/month in payment. APR, closing costs and tax/insurance expenses must be evaluated from actual lender forms if the family proceeds.","The household saves $250 at each month end for 240 months at hypothetical 6% nominal annual interest with monthly compounding. A projected educational expense costs $30,000 now and rises with 3% annual inflation for ten years. The family separately needs $20,000 at each year-end for ten years in a survivor scenario, discounted at a model 4% effective annual rate.","A fictional loan refinancing proposal costs $4,000 now and saves $90 at every month end until the household leaves in 60 months; discount monthly at 0.5%. The family might leave sooner, so test 24 months as well.","A fictional economy records consumption $700b, investment $180b, government spending $210b, exports $90b and imports $120b. Nominal GDP grows 6%, price level grows 3%. Labor data show 950 employed, 50 unemployed seeking work and 200 outside the labor force.","A household economic shock increases essential expenses from $3,000/month to $3,300/month, reduces accessible $12,000 reserves by an unexpected $2,500 deductible, and causes the same $250,000 home price to fall 10% while mortgage principal remains $237,500.","Official macro indicators may be revised after publication, and no hypothetical market yield constitutes a verified mortgage offer. The committee must identify the data release vintage and not misrepresent a yield-curve inversion or GDP quarters as a certain recession."],"tasks":["Compute CPI one-year inflation, second-year level and two-year cumulative inflation; then calculate personal fixed-basket inflation from category weights.","Compute exact real return for the 2% savings account under 5% inflation, and compare $50,000 salary after a hypothetical 3% nominal raise under 5% inflation.","Compare approximate and exact nominal-minus-TIPS break-even inflation and explain why the yield spread is not a pure inflation forecast.","Price the zero-coupon security at 4% and 5%, and use modified duration six to approximate a +75-basis-point shock on the $20,000 bond exposure.","Calculate 30-year P&I on $240,000 at the three supplied rates, the one-point fee, and a simple payment-savings breakeven; distinguish note rates and APR.","Compute twenty-year $250/month ordinary-annuity growth, contributions and hypothetical investment gains, then convert its final value into initial purchasing-power dollars at 3% inflation.","Inflate the $30,000 education target for ten years and compute PV of ten $20,000 survivor annual needs discounted at 4%. State that a life policy face amount is not current bank liquidity.","Calculate the NPV of a $4,000 upfront refinance with $90/month for 60 months at 0.5% per month; compare 24 months and identify omitted terminal-balance factors.","Calculate fictional GDP with expenditure identity, illustrative real GDP growth and annualized rate corresponding to 0.5% one-quarter real growth.","Calculate labor force, U-3 unemployment, participation and employment-to-population rate; describe what happens to the headline rate if 20 seekers stop looking.","Compute household cash runway before and after inflation plus deductible, and leveraged home equity at −10% home price change.","Write a full investment-policy decision memo that prioritizes liquidity and mortgage affordability, distinguishes real and nominal figures, describes data uncertainty and lists at least seven primary sources."],"answers":["CPI 300→312 =4%; next year 312×1.03=321.36; two-year inflation 321.36/300−1=7.12%. Household fixed-basket price change =.45×6%+.20×3%+.15×(−2%)+.10×8%+.10×4%=4.2%.","Exact savings-account real return 1.02/1.05−1≈−2.8571%. $50,000 salary grows to $51,500 nominal; prior-year purchasing-power equivalent $51,500/1.05≈$49,047.62.","Approximate break-even inflation =4.5%−2.0%=2.5%; compounded factor version 1.045/1.02−1≈2.45098%. Term/liquidity and inflation premiums prevent treating this as a guaranteed CPI forecast.","Zero-coupon $1,000/1.04³≈$889.00; at 5% ≈$863.84. Modified duration six with +0.0075 ⇒ ~−4.5% price, or −$900 on $20,000, before convexity/credit/coupon effects.","At $240,000 and 360 monthly payments: 5% payment≈$1,288.37; 6%≈$1,438.92; 7%≈$1,596.73. One point costs $2,400; at $45/month savings, naive break-even≈53.33 months. APR includes specified finance-charge treatment, P&I does not.","Future value C×[(1+r)^n−1]/r with C=$250, r=.005, n=240 ⇒≈$115,510.22. Contributions $60,000; growth≈$55,510.22. At 3% CPI inflation for twenty years, future amount in initial purchasing power ≈$63,955.21, before investment fees/taxes.","Education future cost $30,000×1.03^10≈$40,317.49. Survivor expense PV $20,000×[1−1.04^(−10)]/.04≈$162,217.92. Both discounting and actual insurance coverage remain conditional.","Sixty-month PV of $90 end-month receipts at 0.5% monthly≈$4,655.30; NPV after $4,000 cost≈+$655.30. At 24 months, PV≈$2,257.41 and NPV≈−$1,742.59; loan balance and taxes are not modeled.","GDP=700+180+210+90−120=$1,060 billion. Illustrative real output growth 1.06/1.03−1≈2.9126%. One-quarter 0.5% annualized=(1.005)^4−1≈2.015%.","Labor force 1,000; U-3=50/1000=5%; total modeled eligible population 1,200; participation≈83.333%; employment-to-population≈79.167%. If 20 unemployed exit the labor force, 30/980≈3.061% U-3 without new jobs.","Initial cash runway $12,000/$3,000=4 months; after spending rises to $3,300, 3.636 months; after $2,500 deductible, $9,500/$3,300≈2.879 months. Initial home equity $12,500; −10% home price leaves $225,000−$237,500=−$12,500, before selling costs.","Committee recommendation must prioritize due-date cash, emergency reserves, insured liquidity, real purchasing-power targets, housing all-in costs and contribution affordability. Sources include BLS, BEA, NBER, Fed, TreasuryDirect, CFPB and FRED/ALFRED; economic indicators are not future certainty."],"rubric":["Price index and real/nominal math (20%)","Yield curve, bond valuation and mortgage comparison (20%)","Annuities, survivor PV and refinancing NPV (25%)","Economic measurement and data-vintage methodology (20%)","Household contingency decisions, citations and limitations (15%)"],"sources":[["BLS CPI","https://www.bls.gov/cpi/"],["TreasuryDirect TIPS","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/tips/"],["TreasuryDirect bond pricing","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/understanding-pricing/"],["The Federal Reserve Monetary Policy","https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/monetary-policy.htm"],["CFPB mortgage disclosure","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"],["BEA GDP","https://www.bea.gov/resources/learning-center/what-to-know-gdp"],["NBER Dating","https://www.nber.org/research/business-cycle-dating"],["FRED source series","https://fred.stlouisfed.org/"]]},"limits":{"preservesOriginal":true,"notAccredited":true,"assumptionsHypothetical":true,"modelInputsLocalOnly":true,"sourceDatesRequired":true}};
